# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Oriant — environment template
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  YOU CAN BUILD AND TEST ALL OF M1-M3 WITH AN EMPTY FILE.
#
#  Fixture mode plus file storage — the two defaults — need no configuration at
#  all. No key, no database, no service. Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill
#  in ONLY what the milestone you are on requires. Every variable below is blank
#  on purpose; a blank variable is a supported state, not a broken one.
#
#      cp .env.example .env.local
#
#  `.env.local` is gitignored. This template carries NAMES ONLY, never values.
#
#  EVERY VARIABLE APPEARS EXACTLY ONCE. If you find a name twice, one of them is
#  being ignored and which one depends on load order, so the duplicate is a bug
#  in this file rather than a style question.
#
#  Two variables carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on purpose — NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#  and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY — because the browser-side Supabase client
#  needs them and both are safe to publish. THAT PREFIX INLINES THE VALUE INTO
#  THE BROWSER BUNDLE, so it belongs on those two and on nothing else here. No
#  other variable in this file may be given it.
#
#  Full guide, including which milestone needs what: docs/RUNTIME_SETUP.md
#  Storage decision, schema and limitations:        docs/STORAGE.md
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  A. RUNTIME MODE, STORAGE AND THE SCHEDULER
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE — the single switch that decides whether the agent
# runtime talks to anything outside this process.
#
#   fixture (DEFAULT)  FixtureReasoner + StubToolClient. No network calls and no
#                      keys. Paired with the sandbox's FixedClock and seeded
#                      newId() it is fully deterministic: same input, same
#                      events, same verdict, every time. (Fixture mode alone
#                      removes the network; the clock and id factory are what
#                      remove the remaining variance.) THIS IS THE IMPORTANT ONE —
#                      EVERYTHING IN M1, M2 AND M3 WORKS WITH NO KEYS AT ALL.
#                      Runs, approvals, policy decisions, limit breaches,
#                      pause/resume and sandbox verdicts are all exercised
#                      here. You do not need a single credential to develop,
#                      test or demo the runtime spine.
#
#   live               Real LLM for `reason` steps and real tool clients for
#                      `fetch`/`act` steps. Requires at minimum the AI& block
#                      below, plus whichever tool credentials the workflow
#                      touches.
#
# Required?      No. Unset behaves exactly as `fixture`, and any value other
#                than `live` is treated as `fixture` (fail closed).
# First needed:  M1, and only if you want live reason steps. Never needed for
#                fixture work.
# Breaks without it: nothing.
# Where from:    you. It is a local choice, not a credential.
#
# Note: this is the RUNTIME's switch (lib/runtime, Role C). It is separate from
# the older per-provider fallback in lib/server/providers/env.ts, which powers
# the /demo lane and independently drops to fixture whenever a provider's keys
# are absent. The two do not conflict; they govern different lanes.
ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE=

# ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE — where the runtime keeps what it learns.
#
#   file (DEFAULT)     Unset, blank, or "file". One JSON file per record under
#                      data/runtime/, written atomically. Runs, events,
#                      approvals, decisions, packages, triggers, queued jobs,
#                      deployments and agent state all outlive the process —
#                      which is what makes the approval interrupt real, because
#                      a run that pauses for four hours must not depend on the
#                      process it paused in still being alive. No service, no
#                      migration, no setup step: this is why a clean clone runs
#                      on `npm install && npm run dev`.
#
#   memory             The in-memory stores. Correct, fast, and DISCARDED WITH
#                      THE PROCESS: a paused run and its pending approval are
#                      gone on restart. Reach for it for a throwaway experiment,
#                      never for anything you intend to come back to.
#
#   postgres           THE PRODUCTION PATH. "supabase" is accepted as an alias
#                      for the same store. The same three store interfaces
#                      backed by Supabase Postgres, reading DATABASE_URL from
#                      section B and nothing else. Records travel as jsonb
#                      carrying the SAME tagged encoding the file store uses
#                      (persist/codec.ts), because jsonb has no Date, Map or Set
#                      either — which is what lets a run written by one store be
#                      read by the other.
#
#                      What changes when you switch: run events stop being
#                      embedded in the run and become their own appended table
#                      (docs/STORAGE.md §6.5), and the three properties the file
#                      store enforced by hand become schema properties instead —
#                      an idempotency key is a primary key, an approval decision
#                      is a 1:1 row that cannot be written twice, and claiming a
#                      queued job is `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` rather than a lock
#                      file. Tables are namespaced `oriant_*`, so the runtime can
#                      share a Supabase project with anything else the team puts
#                      there. The DDL (lib/runtime/persist/pg/schema.ts) is all
#                      IF NOT EXISTS, so applying it is idempotent — there is no
#                      migration tool to install and no ordered history to keep.
#
#   anything else      THROWS at session construction. Note that this is the
#                      opposite posture to ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE above, which
#                      shrugs at a value it does not know and picks fixture. The
#                      asymmetry is deliberate: for the reasoner the dangerous
#                      default is "does something", for storage it is "forgets
#                      something". A typo like "postgress" read as "file" would
#                      hand you a green server that is not using the database you
#                      just configured.
#
# Required?      No, and the DEFAULT IS THE DURABLE ONE — you have to ask for
#                the forgetful one by name.
# First needed:  never, to make anything work. M4 onwards depends on durability,
#                and durability is what you get for free. Reach for "postgres"
#                when state has to outlive the filesystem the process runs on:
#                a deployment with an ephemeral disk, or two processes that must
#                see one another's runs and queue claims.
# Breaks without it: nothing. With it set to "postgres" and DATABASE_URL absent,
#                session construction throws an error naming both variables;
#                with DATABASE_URL present but the wrong shape it throws
#                PostgresConfigError from persist/pg/client.ts. Either way it is
#                loud at wiring rather than silent at the first write.
# Where from:    you. A deployment choice, not a credential.
ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE=

# ORIANT_RUNTIME_DATA_DIR — where "file" storage puts those records.
#
# ONLY APPLIES TO ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE=file. Under "memory" and "postgres" it
# is read by nothing, so setting it there changes no behaviour.
#
# Required?      No. Blank means <working directory>/data/runtime. A relative
#                value is resolved against the working directory; an absolute
#                one is taken as given, and absolute is what two processes
#                started from different directories need — otherwise each gets
#                its own private copy of every run and neither sees the other's
#                queue claims.
# First needed:  never. Set it to keep runtime state outside the repo, or to
#                give two checkouts separate state.
# Breaks without it: nothing. data/ is already gitignored, so nothing is
#                committed either way.
# Where from:    you. One caution: this directory is NOT encrypted and NOT
#                access-controlled. Run context holds customer names, invoice
#                amounts and draft email bodies, and it inherits the
#                filesystem's permissions and nothing more — so do not point it
#                at a synced or shared folder (docs/STORAGE.md §6).
ORIANT_RUNTIME_DATA_DIR=

# ORIANT_POLLER — whether this server process turns the scheduler by itself.
#
#   off (DEFAULT)      Unset, blank, "off", "false", "0", "no" or "disabled".
#                      Triggers still register at activation, agents still read
#                      as live, and every schedule still computes its next fire
#                      time — but NOTHING FIRES ON ITS OWN. Each firing waits
#                      for a POST to /api/runtime/scheduler, which is one pass
#                      of the same worker.
#
#   on                 Also "true", "1", "yes", "enabled". A background poller
#                      runs one worker pass every ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS:
#                      due schedules are queued, jobs are claimed, runs start
#                      with nobody watching. This is the switch that turns an
#                      activated plan into a workforce that runs itself.
#
# Required?      No, and OFF IS THE DEFAULT ON PURPOSE — it is the only variable
#                here that makes the process act without being asked. `npm run
#                dev` should serve screens, not start dispatching a workforce
#                thirty seconds later against whatever credentials happen to be
#                in .env.local. In live mode that would be real customer email.
#                Turning it on is a decision somebody writes down.
# First needed:  M4, and only for a deployment that is meant to fire schedules
#                unattended. Never needed to develop, test or demo: the verify
#                suite drives the worker directly and the route drives one pass
#                per request.
# Breaks without it: nothing breaks — a live workforce simply never starts a
#                run on its own. That is the failure worth naming, because it is
#                silent: the Friday sweep does not happen and nobody notices
#                until the invoices are a month old.
# Where from:    you. It is a deployment choice, not a credential.
#
# Anything this build cannot read — a typo like "treu" — is REFUSED rather than
# guessed at: the poller does not start, the server still boots, and the reason
# is printed on stderr with the accepted values beside it. A mistyped switch
# that quietly meant "off" would be the silent failure above wearing a green
# deployment. Details, including what to do when several server processes share
# one queue: docs/RUNTIME_SETUP.md §3b.
ORIANT_POLLER=

# ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS — milliseconds between passes, e.g. 5000.
#
# Required?      No. Blank means 30000 (30s), which is the right order of
#                magnitude for cron: a TriggerSpec schedule is minute-granular,
#                so polling faster buys latency on a demo and nothing else.
#                Whole milliseconds only, and values under 1000 are refused —
#                below a second is a busy loop against the job store, and is far
#                more likely to be seconds typed into a milliseconds field.
#                Read only when ORIANT_POLLER is on.
ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS=

# ORIANT_EVENTS_INTERVAL_MS — milliseconds between store observations on the
# live-updates stream (/api/runtime/events), which is what pushes a new approval
# into the inbox without the owner refreshing.
#
# Not the same clock as ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS above. That one decides how
# often the worker LOOKS FOR WORK; this one decides how often the server looks
# for CHANGES TO REPORT. A fast poller with a slow stream runs agents promptly
# and tells nobody.
#
# Required?      No. Blank means 3000 (3s).
# First needed:  M7, and only to tune it. Never needed to run anything.
# Breaks without it: nothing.
# Bounds:        under 500 and over 300000 are REFUSED, not clamped. Below half
#                a second a pass is a busy loop over the whole run store; past
#                five minutes the word "realtime" is not true and the client's
#                own fallback poll would serve the owner better.
# Where from:    you. It is a tuning choice, not a credential.
#
# Read once at module scope, so a change needs a server restart.
ORIANT_EVENTS_INTERVAL_MS=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  B. SUPABASE  —  THE DATABASE AND THE PROJECT KEYS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  FOUR VARIABLES, TWO DIFFERENT DOORS INTO THE SAME PROJECT, AND MIXING THEM UP
#  IS THE MISTAKE THIS SECTION EXISTS TO PREVENT.
#
#    DATABASE_URL                   a Postgres wire-protocol connection string.
#                                   postgresql://…:6543/postgres
#    NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL       the project's HTTPS API origin.
#                                   https://<ref>.supabase.co
#
#  They point at the same project and are NOT interchangeable. Pasting the
#  project URL into DATABASE_URL is by a wide margin the commonest error, so the
#  connection code refuses that value by name rather than failing obscurely.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# DATABASE_URL — the Postgres connection string the runtime stores use.
#
# READ ONLY WHEN ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE=postgres. Under "file" (the default) and
# "memory" nothing reads it, and setting it changes no behaviour whatsoever.
#
# It must be the Supabase TRANSACTION POOLER URI, which looks like this:
#
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
#
# Two facts about that host decide the shape:
#
#   THE POOLER, NOT THE DIRECT HOST. `db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432` is the direct
#   connection: IPv6-only on current projects, and one backend per client, which
#   a serverless function multiplies until the project runs out of connections.
#   It is fine for a local one-off and wrong for anything deployed. The client
#   warns when it sees that host rather than refusing it, because only you know
#   which of the two you are doing.
#
#   THE POOLER FORBIDS PREPARED STATEMENTS. A pooled connection is handed to a
#   different client between statements, so `prepare: false` is set for you. You
#   do not configure this; it is named here because it is why the pooler URI is
#   the supported one and why query code in this repo never relies on prepares.
#
# DO NOT PUT THE PROJECT URL HERE. `https://<ref>.supabase.co` is not a
# connection string — it is the REST/API origin, and it belongs in
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL below. This is the single most common way to get a
# database that will not connect, and it has already been done once in this
# repo, so the connection code detects an http(s):// value and says exactly this
# instead of letting the driver produce something unreadable.
#
# Required?      Only when ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE=postgres. Then it is REQUIRED
#                and there is no fallback: the runtime will not quietly drop to
#                file storage, because a database you configured and are not
#                using is the failure this build refuses to have.
# First needed:  M4 in practice — the first deployment whose state must outlive
#                the container. Never needed to develop, test or demo.
# Breaks without it: with storage set to postgres, session construction throws
#                before any run starts — an error naming both variables when it
#                is absent, PostgresConfigError when it is present but the wrong
#                shape. With storage on its default nothing breaks at all.
# Where from:    Supabase dashboard → Connect → Transaction pooler → URI. Copy
#                it whole, then replace the literal [YOUR-PASSWORD] with the
#                database password (Settings → Database). Leaving the
#                placeholder in is detected and refused by name. URL-encode the
#                password if it contains @ : / ? # or %.
#
# Use a database that is not shared with demo data. To wipe runtime state, drop
# the `oriant_*` tables — nothing else in the project shares that prefix.
#
# The legacy /demo lane keeps using the data/db.json file store and ignores this
# variable entirely.
#
# Check it before you rely on it:  npm run db:check
# That does a REAL round trip — migrate, insert, read back, delete — because "the
# connection opened" is not the same claim as "this database can store a paused
# run". It walks the failures named above in order and prints the sentence for
# whichever one it hits, and exits non-zero so CI can gate on it. The connection
# string is never printed: only its scheme, host class, port, and whether a
# password is present.
DATABASE_URL=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL — the project's API origin, e.g.
# https://<ref>.supabase.co
#
# BROWSER-EXPOSED, AND THAT IS CORRECT. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix inlines this
# value into the client bundle, which is exactly what the anon client needs and
# exactly why this variable must never hold a secret. It is a public address,
# the same one anybody can read out of the network tab.
#
# Required?      Required for anything that talks to Supabase over HTTP —
#                onboarding and planner persistence, org membership, uploaded
#                assets, auth. NOT required by the runtime's Postgres stores;
#                those go over the wire protocol via DATABASE_URL.
# First needed:  the first screen that reads or writes Supabase-backed data.
# Breaks without it: the Supabase client cannot be constructed, so every
#                Supabase-backed screen fails at its first call. Runtime storage
#                is unaffected either way.
# Where from:    Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → Project URL.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY — the anon (publishable) key.
#
# BROWSER-EXPOSED, AND THAT IS CORRECT. This key is designed to ship to the
# client. It grants nothing on its own: every table it touches is gated by
# row-level security, so what a holder can actually read or write is whatever
# your RLS policies allow for their session. Which means RLS is the security
# boundary — if a table has no policy, publishing this key publishes the table.
#
# Required?      Yes, together with NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL. One without the
#                other is not a usable client.
# First needed:  the first screen that reads or writes Supabase-backed data.
# Breaks without it: same as above — no Supabase client, so no Supabase-backed
#                screen works. The runtime is unaffected.
# Where from:    Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → anon / public
#                (newer projects label it "publishable").
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY — the service role key. SERVER-ONLY.
#
# THIS KEY BYPASSES ROW-LEVEL SECURITY COMPLETELY. It reads and writes every row
# in every table regardless of policy, which is why it exists and why it is
# dangerous: it is the one credential here for which "leaked" means "the whole
# database was read". Say it plainly — a browser holding this key is a database
# with no access control.
#
# IT MUST NEVER BE GIVEN A NEXT_PUBLIC_ PREFIX. That prefix inlines the value
# into the client bundle, where it is permanent, public and unrevokable without
# rotating the key. If you ever find yourself typing
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, the thing you actually want is the anon
# key above plus an RLS policy.
#
# Required?      OPTIONAL. Only server-side work that must legitimately act
#                across tenants needs it — admin/back-office paths, migrations,
#                background jobs writing on a user's behalf.
# First needed:  the first server route that has to write past RLS. Not needed
#                for the runtime's Postgres stores, which connect as the
#                database user in DATABASE_URL, not through the API.
# Breaks without it: those specific server paths fail; everything RLS-gated
#                keeps working through the anon key.
# Where from:    Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role
#                (revealed behind a click, because of the above).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  C. AI&  —  the agent brain
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: an OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint. It powers every
# `reason` step in a workflow — deciding which overdue invoices to chase,
# drafting the reminder text, judging whether a thread needs a human. The
# runtime never lets the model choose a tool: control flow stays in the
# executor and the model is confined to `reason` steps. It also backs
# company-report generation, workforce planning and natural-language plan edits.
#
# Required?      REQUIRED for live mode. Irrelevant in fixture mode.
# First needed:  M1 (runtime core), the moment you set ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE=live.
# Breaks without it: in live mode the AiAndReasoner constructor throws
#                ReasonerConfigError, naming the missing variables, before any
#                run starts — loud at wiring rather than silent at the first
#                `reason` step. Fixture mode is completely unaffected;
#                FixtureReasoner returns canned, deterministic results.
# Where from:    your AI& console. Base URL and model id are org-specific;
#                model access varies per organisation, so copy both from the
#                console rather than guessing.
#
# All three must be present together. One or two of the three is treated as
# not configured.
AIAND_API_KEY=
AIAND_BASE_URL=
AIAND_MODEL=

# Supabase — Role A onboarding persistence, org membership, auditability and
# uploaded onboarding assets. Keep the service role key server-side only.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  D. OpenAI  —  alternative / additional LLM provider
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: a direct OpenAI key, for the places where an OpenAI-specific model
# or tool is wanted rather than the OpenAI-COMPATIBLE endpoint AI& exposes. AI&
# stays the primary brain; this is the second provider, not a replacement for
# the first.
#
# Required?      OPTIONAL. Nothing in M1-M3 reads it, and live mode does not
#                need it — live mode needs the AI& block in section C.
# First needed:  the first feature that asks for OpenAI by name. If you are not
#                sure whether you have one, you do not need this key.
# Breaks without it: that one feature falls back to AI& or to fixture output and
#                is labelled as such. Nothing else changes; a `reason` step
#                never touches this key.
# Where from:    platform.openai.com → API keys. Billing must be enabled on the
#                organisation or the key authenticates and then 429s on the
#                first call.
OPENAI_API_KEY=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  E. Doubleword  —  agent package generation
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: async/batch OpenAI-compatible inference used by the Agent Factory
# to compile an `AgentSpec` into an `AgentPackage` (system prompt, per-workflow
# prompt fragments, resolved tool bindings, flattened operation allowlist) —
# one job per preset personalisation or custom agent.
#
# This only powers transcribing YOUR answers. Margo's own speaking voice is
# the browser's Web Speech API and needs no key.
NOSANA_WHISPER_URL=
NOSANA_API_KEY=

# Doubleword — async/batch OpenAI-compatible inference.
# Used for: asynchronous agent-package generation (one job per agent).
# If your org console shows different async endpoint paths, adjust the two
# constants at the top of lib/server/providers/doubleword.ts.
DOUBLEWORD_API_KEY=
DOUBLEWORD_BASE_URL=
DOUBLEWORD_MODEL=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  F. Daytona  —  sandbox isolation
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: a remote sandbox that a scenario is executed INSIDE. The runner is
# bundled (npm run sandbox:bundle), uploaded, and run there; the ScenarioResult
# comes back over stdout. Nothing about the scenario runs on this machine, which
# is the only version of "isolation" worth the name — wrapping a local call in a
# remote handle would report a remote verdict for local work.
#
# Required?      OPTIONAL, and OPT-IN. Nothing reads these unless a caller asks
#                for the Daytona isolation by name. `npm run verify` and
#                `npm run verify:m3` stay in process on purpose: the M3 exit
#                criterion is a byte-identical verdict across five consecutive
#                runs, and 24 network round trips per run would make that slow
#                and hostage to a DNS hiccup.
# First needed:  M3, and only when you want the isolation guarantee — a future
#                generator that emits executable code, or proving to someone
#                else that nothing escaped. Package validation may also be
#                triggered from the planner/build handoff.
# Breaks without it: you lose ISOLATION, nothing else. Scenario execution,
#                approval checkpoints, pass rates and verdicts all work
#                locally. Determinism does NOT depend on this key — it comes
#                from the injected Clock, seeded newId() and stubbed tools, and
#                a remotely executed scenario is byte-identical to a local one
#                for exactly that reason. If it ever is not, that is a bug in
#                isolation, not a tolerance to widen.
# Where from:    app.daytona.io → Keys. The key needs sandbox CREATE and DELETE
#                permission; without create it authenticates fine and then fails
#                403 on the first sandbox. DAYTONA_API_URL defaults to
#                https://app.daytona.io/api and only needs setting for a
#                self-hosted or regional endpoint.
#
# One other thing reads DAYTONA_API_KEY, and it can no longer use it. The legacy
# /demo lane's validator (lib/server/providers/daytona.ts → ValidateScreen)
# hand-rolled its own transport, and the endpoint it called now 404s: the toolbox
# moved behind proxy.app.daytona.io, which rejects an organisation key. That
# adapter no longer attempts the call — it runs its checks locally, labels them
# `fixture`, and says on the manifest that this was not a sandbox run. Setting
# the key does not give the /demo lane isolation; only lib/runtime/sandbox/remote
# has that, and only when a caller asks for it.
#
# Check it before you rely on it:  npm run daytona:check
# That creates and deletes one real sandbox, prints presence-and-length for the
# key (never the key), and exits non-zero with one actionable sentence when
# isolation is unavailable. The two failures a fresh organisation hits are named
# there exactly: "no default region" (400) and "Access denied" (403).
DAYTONA_API_KEY=
DAYTONA_API_URL=
